q(u,2008,3,'Combinatorics',r'For a positive integer $n$, let $S_n=\{(x,y)\in\mathbb Z^2:|x|+|y+\tfrac12|<n\}$. A path is a sequence of distinct points of $S_n$ whose successive points are at distance $1$. Prove that partitioning $S_n$ into paths requires at least $n$ paths.','Graph theory|Extremal arguments|Induction','A boundary extremal path must be rerouted before a strip can be removed inductively. Keeping components distinct and avoiding cycles makes the induction much subtler than a simple colouring count.')
q(u,2009,3,'Combinatorics',r'A simple polygon with integer-coordinate horizontal and vertical sides is tiled by unit squares coloured alternately black and white. Call a domino tiling tasteful if it has neither of these patterns: a $2\times2$ block with white lower-left square covered by two horizontal dominoes, or a $2\times2$ block with black lower-left square covered by two vertical dominoes. Prove that every such polygon admitting a domino tiling has exactly one tasteful tiling. The polygon has no holes.','Tilings & coverings|Induction|Graph theory','Existence uses a tiling-preserving lower-left induction. Uniqueness compares alternating cycles from two tilings and propagates a staircase until its boundary orientation contradicts the forbidden patterns.')
q(u,2015,6,'Algebra',r'Fix $0<\lambda<1$. A multiset $A$ of positive integers satisfies $|A_n|\le\lambda n$ for every positive integer $n$, where $A_n$ consists of its entries at most $n$, counted with multiplicity. Prove that infinitely many $n$ satisfy $\sum_{a\in A_n}a\le\lambda n(n+1)/2$.','Sequences & recurrences|Sums & products|Inequalities','The density bound controls counts but not each partial sum. Summation by parts and an extremal or limiting argument are needed to force infinitely many good endpoints.')
q(u,2016,3,'Geometry',r'Let $ABC$ be acute, with circumcentre $O$ and excentres $I_B,I_C$. The $B$-angle bisector and altitude meet $AC$ at $Y,E$, respectively; the $C$-angle bisector and altitude meet $AB$ at $Z,F$. If $P=I_BF\cap I_CE$, prove $PO\perp YZ$.','Triangle centres|Coordinates & vectors|Trigonometry','The excentres, altitude feet and angle-bisector traces do not yield a direct angle chase. A substantial coordinate or ratio calculation must expose the perpendicularity.')
q(u,2017,2,'Combinatorics',r'Fix a multiset of $n$ positive integers. For an integer sequence $A=(a_1,\ldots,a_n)$, call a pair $i<j$ an $A$-inversion of a permutation $w$ if $a_i\ge w_i>w_j$, or $w_j>a_i\ge w_i$, or $w_i>w_j>a_i$. Prove that for every positive integer $k$, the number of multiset permutations with $k$ such inversions is independent of $A$.','Permutations & arrangements|Bijections|Induction','The unusual cyclic ordering in the inversion definition demands a bijection or an inductive generating-function argument that works with repeated entries and changing thresholds.')
q(u,2017,3,'Geometry',r'In a scalene triangle $ABC$ with circumcircle $\Omega$ and incentre $I$, ray $AI$ meets $BC$ at $D$ and $\Omega$ again at $M$. The circle of diameter $DM$ meets $\Omega$ again at $K$. Set $S=MK\cap BC$ and let $N$ be the midpoint of $IS$. If the circles $(KID)$ and $(MAN)$ meet at $L_1,L_2$, prove that $\Omega$ contains the midpoint of $IL_1$ or of $IL_2$.','Circles & tangency|Triangle centres|Coordinates & vectors','The required midpoint is not directly visible in the many-circle configuration; a carefully chosen coordinate model and identification of the two intersection branches are needed.')
q(u,2017,5,'Combinatorics',r'Find all real $c>0$ for which the integer lattice can be labelled with positive integers, using only finitely many distinct labels, so that any two points labelled $i$ are at distance at least $c^i$.','Colouring|Combinatorial geometry|Algorithms & constructions','A density obstruction must be matched by a finite hierarchical lattice colouring. The exponent attached to each label makes ordinary periodic colouring insufficient without a careful construction.')
q(u,2018,3,'Number Theory',r'Let $n\ge2$, $m=\varphi(n)$, and let $a_1,\ldots,a_m$ be the positive integers less than $n$ coprime to $n$. Suppose every prime divisor of $m$ divides $n$. Prove $m\mid\sum_{i=1}^{m}a_i^k$ for every positive integer $k$.','Modular arithmetic|Valuations|Primes','The full totient divisibility must be established prime-power by prime-power. Grouping the reduced residues and handling their power sums is the main arithmetic step.')
q(u,2018,5,'Geometry',r'Let $ABCD$ be convex and cyclic, with $E=AC\cap BD$, $F=AB\cap CD$, and $G=DA\cap BC$. Circle $(ABE)$ meets $CB$ again at $P$; circle $(ADE)$ meets $CD$ again at $Q$. Assume $C,B,P,G$ and $C,Q,D,F$ occur on their lines in those orders. For $M=FP\cap GQ$, prove $MA\perp AC$.','Power of a point|Coordinates & vectors|Circles & tangency','The intersection data form a complete quadrilateral, but exploiting it requires several power and ratio identities. The specified orders also matter in converting these to the final orthogonality.')
q(u,2018,6,'Combinatorics',r'Let $a_n$ count permutations $(x_1,\ldots,x_n)$ of $(1,\ldots,n)$ for which all ratios $x_i/i$ are distinct. Prove that $a_n$ is odd for every positive integer $n$.','Permutations & arrangements|Parity|Bijections','A parity proof must pair the inadmissible or admissible permutations while understanding the fixed configurations. The ratio constraint makes a naive reversal pairing ineffective.')
q(u,2019,3,'Number Theory',r'Let $K$ be the positive integers whose decimal expansions omit digit $7$. Determine all real polynomials with nonnegative coefficients such that $f(x)\in K$ whenever $x\in K$.','Polynomials|Digits & bases|Algorithms & constructions','The digit restriction must force severe algebraic restrictions on the polynomial. Large separated decimal blocks and carry control are needed, rather than checking a few residue classes.')
q(u,2019,6,'Algebra',r'Find all real polynomials $P$ satisfying $\dfrac{P(x)}{yz}+\dfrac{P(y)}{zx}+\dfrac{P(z)}{xy}=P(x-y)+P(y-z)+P(z-x)$ whenever nonzero reals $x,y,z$ satisfy $2xyz=x+y+z$.','Polynomials|Functional equations|Algebraic identities','The constraint prevents arbitrary substitutions. Suitable parametrizations and leading-degree comparisons eliminate possible high-degree terms before the remaining coefficients can be classified.')
q(u,2021,3,'Combinatorics',r'An empty $n\times n$ board, $n\ge2$, allows three operations: place stones in three empty cells forming a translate of $\{(0,0),(1,0),(0,1)\}$, without rotation; remove all stones from a full row; or remove all stones from a full column. Determine the $n$ for which a nonempty finite sequence of operations returns to an empty board.','Invariants|Linear algebra|Polynomials','A construction is possible in blocks, but the obstruction outside the admissible sizes needs a nontrivial algebraic invariant or matrix calculation involving roots of unity.')
q(u,2021,6,'Geometry',r'A convex hexagon $ABCDEF$ satisfies $AB\parallel DE$, $BC\parallel EF$, $CD\parallel FA$, and $AB\cdot DE=BC\cdot EF=CD\cdot FA$. Let $X,Y,Z$ be the midpoints of $AD,BE,CF$. Prove that the circumcentres of $ACE$ and $BDF$ and the orthocentre of $XYZ$ are collinear.','Coordinates & vectors|Triangle centres|Concurrency & collinearity','The parallel-side product conditions encode a special affine relation between the alternating triangles. Translating that relation into centre coordinates is the difficult bridge to collinearity.')
q(u,2022,3,'Algebra',r'Find all functions $f:(0,\infty)\to(0,\infty)$ satisfying $f(x)=f(f(f(x))+y)+f(xf(y))f(x+y)$ for all positive $x,y$.','Functional equations|Inequalities','Positivity yields useful strict inequalities, but the nested arguments require a global bounding and comparison argument before the function can be pinned down.')
q(u,2022,6,'Combinatorics',r'A network has $2022$ users and mutual permanent friendships. A new friendship may be added only when its two users already have at least two common friends. Find the smallest possible initial number of friendships that allows the network eventually to become complete.','Graph theory|Invariants|Algorithms & constructions','A sharp construction is only one side of the problem; proving the matching lower bound requires tracking an invariant through the graph-completion process.')
q(u,2023,3,'Combinatorics',r'For odd $n$, tile all but one cell of an $n\times n$ board with dominoes, giving a configuration $C$. A move slides one domino, without rotation, into the empty cell and leaves another cell empty. Let $k(C)$ count all configurations reachable by such moves, including $C$. Determine every possible value of $k(C)$ in terms of $n$.','Tilings & coverings|Graph theory|Invariants','The empty-cell motion has a hidden graph structure. One must characterize reachable states and construct configurations realizing every permitted size, not merely bound the number of holes.')
q(u,2023,6,'Geometry',r'Triangle $ABC$ has incentre $I$ and excentres $I_a,I_b,I_c$. Choose $D$ on its circumcircle, off lines $II_a,I_bI_c,BC$, so that circles $(DII_a)$ and $(DI_bI_c)$ meet again at $F\ne D$. If $E=DF\cap BC$, prove $\angle BAD=\angle EAC$.','Triangle centres|Power of a point|Circles & tangency','The common chord of two excentral circles must be related to an isogonal line through A. Radical-axis identities and the in/excentre geometry supply the essential connection.')
q(u,2024,2,'Combinatorics',r'Finite integer sets $S_1,\ldots,S_{100}$ have a nonempty common intersection. For every nonempty subcollection of $k$ of these sets, its intersection size is divisible by $k$. Find the least possible number of elements belonging to at least $50$ sets.','Sets & partitions|Counting|Divisibility & GCD','The intersection divisibility constraints are coupled across all subset sizes. Inclusion–exclusion or a suitable counting basis is required, followed by a construction meeting the bound.')
q(u,2024,3,'Geometry',r'For which positive integers $m,n$ with $n\ge3$ can a regular $n$-gon be triangulated into $n-2$ triangles and those triangles coloured with $m$ colours so that each colour has the same total area?','Area & dissection|Quadrilaterals & polygons|Algorithms & constructions','Necessity requires algebraic restrictions on areas in a regular polygon, while sufficiency needs compatible triangulations. The equality of total colour areas is much weaker than equal individual triangles.')
q(u,2026,6,'Number Theory',r'Positive integers $a,b$ satisfy $\varphi(ab+1)\mid a^2+b^2+1$. Prove that both $a,b$ are Fibonacci numbers.','Diophantine equations|Primes|Infinite descent','Euler-totient restrictions first force a rigid equation, after which a Vieta-style descent reaches the Fibonacci recurrence. The exceptional pair(1,1) needs separate attention in parity arguments.')
q(j,2011,1,'Number Theory',r'Find all positive integers $n$ for which $2^n+12^n+2011^n$ is a square.','Modular arithmetic|Squares & powers','Separate even n from odd n≥3; reduction modulo3 and modulo4 excludes them, leaving the directly checked case n=1.')
q(j,2018,1,'Combinatorics',r'For each positive integer $n$, count the $n$-digit positive integers whose adjacent digits are all unequal and whose last digit is prime.','Counting|Digits & bases|Recurrences & generating functions','A leading-zero comparison yields a short recurrence; the initial condition and its alternating correction must be handled to give the exact count.')
q(j,2023,3,'Combinatorics',r'For odd $n$, tile all but one cell of an $n\times n$ board with dominoes, giving a configuration $C$. A move slides one domino without rotation into the empty cell. Let $k(C)$ count configurations reachable by any finite sequence of slides, including $C$. Find the greatest possible $k(C)$.','Tilings & coverings|Graph theory|Invariants','The empty cell alone does not immediately identify a reachable state. The hidden graph controlling slides gives both the upper bound and a construction attaining it.')
q(j,2024,1,'Geometry',r'In cyclic quadrilateral $ABCD$, $AB=7$ and $CD=8$. Points $P,Q\in AB$ satisfy $AP=BQ=3$, while $R,S\in CD$ satisfy $CR=DS=2$. Prove that $P,Q,R,S$ are concyclic.','Power of a point|Circles & tangency','All four points have the same power−12 with respect to the original circle, so they lie on a concentric circle. Recognizing this reduces the proof to one observation.')
q(j,2024,3,'Number Theory',r'Define $a_1=2$ and $a_{n+1}=a_n^{n+1}-1$ for $n\ge1$. Prove that for every odd prime $p$ and positive integer $k$, some term is divisible by $p^k$.','Sequences & recurrences|Modular arithmetic|Valuations','The exponent varies with the index, so periodicity is not immediate. A careful modular recurrence argument must first force divisibility and then lift it to arbitrary prime powers.')
q(j,2025,3,'Combinatorics',r'For positive integers $m,n$, count paths along the grid edges from the lower-left to the upper-right corner of a $2m\times2n$ rectangle, using only upward and rightward steps, such that each of the two resulting regions, possibly empty, can be tiled by dominoes.','Tilings & coverings|Counting|Bijections','Checkerboard balance gives a necessary condition but must be shown sufficient for both staircase regions. The resulting path condition then needs an exact combinatorial count.')
q(j,2025,4,'Algebra',r'For integers $a_0\ge a_1\ge\cdots\ge a_n\ge0$, prove $\displaystyle\sum_{i=0}^{n}i\binom{a_i}{2}\le\frac12\binom{a_0+\cdots+a_n}{2}$.','Inequalities|Counting|Binomial coefficients','Expand the binomial coefficients and use the monotone ordering to bound each weighted square by cross terms. The proof is a short structured summation once the right pairing is seen.')
q(j,2025,6,'Number Theory',r'A set $S$ of integers contains $1,2,\ldots,2025$. Whenever coprime $a,b$ belong to $S$, so does $ab$. Whenever $s\in S$ and $s+1$ is composite, all positive divisors of $s+1$ belong to $S$. Prove that $S$ contains every positive integer.','Primes|Divisibility & GCD|Induction','The closure rules only directly generate coprime products and divisors of composite successors. A least-missing-number argument must overcome prime powers and the composite-successor restriction.')
q(j,2026,3,'Geometry',r'In an acute scalene triangle $ABC$ with no $60^\circ$ angle, let $\omega$ be its circumcircle. The equilateral triangle inscribed in $\omega$ with vertex $B$ has opposite side-line $\ell_B$; define $\ell_C$ similarly. Set $Y=AC\cap\ell_B$, $Z=AB\cap\ell_C$. Circle $(AYZ)$ meets $\omega$ again at $P$. Put $D=BC\cap YZ$ and $E=PA\cap YZ$. Prove $PE=PD$.','Circles & tangency|Trigonometry|Coordinates & vectors','The fixed equilateral chords impose special trigonometric relations, but transferring them through the two circle intersections and the complete quadrilateral requires a long coordinated argument.')
